SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Colloquium: Vaishnavi Tripuraneni
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Vaishnavi Tripuraneni (she/her) is an Assistant Professo
 r in the department of Geography and Spatial Sciences at the University o
 f Delaware (UD). She is a political ecologist whose work draws on framewo
 rks from agrarian political economy\, human geography\, feminist approach
 es to people-environment interactions\, and human-dimensions of environme
 ntal change. Using mixed methods\, she has conducted extensive research i
 n south India on agrarian debt and biodiversity conservation in human-man
 aged landscapes\, human dimensions of invasive plant management in Califo
 rnia\, and climate resilience in Delaware.  Prior to joining UD\, she was
  a postdoctoral scholar in the Human Dimensions Lab\, Purdue University. 
 She has a Ph.D. in Environmental Studies from the University of Wisconsin
 -Madison. At UD\, Tripuraneni serves as Director of the Environmental Stu
 dies undergraduate program. She teaches human geography and environmental
  studies courses and applies community-engaged teaching and learning peda
 gogy to provide students real-world experience while addressing local\, p
 lace-based\, issues identified by the community.Title  Life and Debt: pro
 duction and reproduction in agrarian south IndiaAbstract  Debt\, a fundam
 ental part of agrarian life and a key contributor to agrarian change\, un
 dermines farmer autonomy\, limits future farm investments\, and sets the 
 emotional and economic conditions of households. Farmers juggle debts to 
 meet not just agricultural needs but also everyday household reproductive
  expenses\, from medical bills to marriage expenses\, in ways not well un
 derstood. This research explores the relationship between productive and 
 reproductive debt in rural Telangana\, India. Life and debt (oral) histor
 ies and surveys of farmers capture experiences of indebtedness and strugg
 les to repay loans. The analysis shows that: 1) reproductive debt is a si
 gnificant part of overall debt\; 2) productive and reproductive debt are 
 intertwined in complicated ways\; and 3) these entanglements lead to casc
 ading patterns of debt\, including borrowing to repay previous loans. The
 se together point to the financialization of reproduction in the agrarian
  household\, with implications for lending\, social service provision\, a
 nd smallholder survival.About the Colloquium SeriesEach Monday throughout
  the academic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research ech
 oing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range f
 rom the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to th
 e environment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Present
 ers are from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zoo
 m to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information\
 , see ourwebsite (https://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html).
