 ntrast="none">Bio</span></strong></p><p>  Vaishnavi Tripuraneni (she/her)
  is an Assistant Professor in the department of Geography and Spatial Sci
 ences at the University of Delaware (UD). She is a political ecologist wh
 ose work draws on frameworks from agrarian political economy\, human geog
 raphy\, feminist approaches to people-environment interactions\, and huma
 n-dimensions of environmental change. Using mixed methods\, she has condu
 cted extensive research in south India on agrarian debt and biodiversity 
 conservation in human-managed landscapes\, human dimensions of invasive p
 lant management in California\, and climate resilience in Delaware.</p><p
 >  Prior to joining UD\, she was a postdoctoral scholar in the Human Dime
 nsions Lab\, Purdue University. She has a Ph.D. in Environmental Studies 
 from the University of Wisconsin-Madison. At UD\, Tripuraneni serves as D
 irector of the Environmental Studies undergraduate program. She teaches h
 uman geography and environmental studies courses and applies community-en
 gaged teaching and learning pedagogy to provide students real-world exper
 ience while addressing local\, place-based\, issues identified by the com
 munity.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="none">Title</span></strong></p
 ><p>  Life and Debt: production and reproduction in agrarian south India<
 /p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  Debt\, a fundamental part of agra
 rian life and a key contributor to agrarian change\, undermines farmer au
 tonomy\, limits future farm investments\, and sets the emotional and econ
 omic conditions of households. Farmers juggle debts to meet not just agri
 cultural needs but also everyday household reproductive expenses\, from m
 edical bills to marriage expenses\, in ways not well understood. This res
 earch explores the relationship between productive and reproductive debt 
 in rural Telangana\, India. Life and debt (oral) histories and surveys of
  farmers capture experiences of indebtedness and struggles to repay loans
 . The analysis shows that: 1) reproductive debt is a significant part of 
 overall debt\; 2) productive and reproductive debt are intertwined in com
 plicated ways\; and 3) these entanglements lead to cascading patterns of 
 debt\, including borrowing to repay previous loans. These together point 
 to the financialization of reproduction in the agrarian household\, with 
 implications for lending\, social service provision\, and smallholder sur
 vival.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="none">About the Colloquium Seri
 es</span></strong></p><p><span data-contrast="none">Each Monday throughou
 t the academic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research ec
 hoing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range 
 from the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to t
 he environment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Presen
 ters are from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zo
 om to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information
 \, see our</span><span data-contrast="none"><a class="external" href="htt
 ps://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener
 ">website</a></span><span data-contrast="none">.</span></p>
