SUMMARY:Virtual Workshop: Polycentric Public Insurance and Climate Adapta
 tion in Switzerland
DESCRIPTION:As climate losses mount and private insurers retreat from vul
 nerable areas worldwide\, the questions of who bears the risk and who act
 s on adaptation are becoming urgent. This talk presents findings from a s
 tudy\, conducted with Rebecca Bednarek and Konstantinos Chalkias\, explor
 ing how Switzerland's nineteen cantonal public-sector disaster insurers c
 an provide compulsory\, affordable\, multiperil coverage without governme
 nt subsidy while actively investing in prevention and mitigation. We prop
 ose that mechanisms familiar to Ostrom scholars — reciprocity\, polycen
 tricity\, and self-regulation — can be extended to explain how governme
 nt actors achieve what cross-sectoral partnerships often cannot\, challen
 ging the dominant view that the public sector should steer rather than ro
 w in addressing grand challenges.Eugenia Cacciatori is Reader (Senior Ass
 ociate Professor) in Management at Bayes Business School\, City St George
 's\, University of London\, researching technological and organizational 
 innovation\, professional knowledge\, and the governance of complex socie
 tal challenges. Her recent research explores disaster insurance and AI ad
 option. Her work appears in journals such as the Academy of Management Jo
 urnal\, Organization Studies\, and Research Policy.Paula Jarzabkowski is 
 Professor of Strategic Management at the University of Queensland and Bay
 es Business School. A Fellow of the British Academy and of the Academy of
  the Social Sciences in Australia\, she is a leading authority on the ins
 urance protection gap and disaster resilience\, and serves on the OECD Hi
 gh-Level Advisory Board for the Financial Management of Catastrophic Risk
 . She received the Academy of Management's Joanne Martin Trailblazer Awar
 d for her foundational work on Strategy-as-Practice.  —  This colloquiu
 m will start with 20 minutes of introductory remarks\, followed by a disc
 ussion moderated by Greg Bloom\, visiting scholar at the Ostrom Workshop 
 and co-founder of the Climate Resilient Insurance Strategy Project (CRISP
 ).
