 ge/gid/105/width/609/height/333/32149_CJ_snip.rev.1787580110.jpg" title="
 CJ snip" data-max-w="827" data-max-h="452" loading="lazy" data-optimized=
 "true"/></picture></p><p>  As climate losses mount and private insurers r
 etreat from vulnerable areas worldwide\, the questions of who bears the r
 isk and who acts on adaptation are becoming urgent. This talk presents fi
 ndings from a study\, conducted with Rebecca Bednarek and Konstantinos Ch
 alkias\, exploring how Switzerland's nineteen cantonal public-sector disa
 ster insurers can provide compulsory\, affordable\, multiperil coverage w
 ithout government subsidy while actively investing in prevention and miti
 gation. We propose that mechanisms familiar to Ostrom scholars — recipr
 ocity\, polycentricity\, and self-regulation — can be extended to expla
 in how government actors achieve what cross-sectoral partnerships often c
 annot\, challenging the dominant view that the public sector should steer
  rather than row in addressing grand challenges.</p><p><strong>Eugenia Ca
 cciatori</strong> is Reader (Senior Associate Professor) in Management at
  Bayes Business School\, City St George's\, University of London\, resear
 ching technological and organizational innovation\, professional knowledg
 e\, and the governance of complex societal challenges. Her recent researc
 h explores disaster insurance and AI adoption. Her work appears in journa
 ls such as the <em>Academy of Management Journal</em>\, <em>Organization 
 Studies</em>\, and <em>Research Policy</em>.</p><p><strong>Paula Jarzabko
 wski</strong> is Professor of Strategic Management at the University of Q
 ueensland and Bayes Business School. A Fellow of the British Academy and 
 of the Academy of the Social Sciences in Australia\, she is a leading aut
 hority on the insurance protection gap and disaster resilience\, and serv
 es on the OECD High-Level Advisory Board for the Financial Management of 
 Catastrophic Risk. She received the Academy of Management's Joanne Martin
  Trailblazer Award for her foundational work on Strategy-as-Practice.</p>
 <p>  —</p><p>  This colloquium will start with 20 minutes of introducto
 ry remarks\, followed by a discussion moderated by Greg Bloom\, visiting 
 scholar at the Ostrom Workshop and co-founder of the Climate Resilient In
 surance Strategy Project (CRISP).</p>
